course: dictionary
lesson: 173
title: Strong feelings: agony
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Agony is a noun. It means very great pain of the body, or very great worry or sadness of the mind. «She lay in agony after she twisted her ankle» shows real pain, and the words «in agony» are the clue.
explain: Read the clue words around it. «The waiting was agony!» has no real pain in it. The exclamation mark and the word waiting tell you that agony here means something very hard or annoying. Ask yourself: is someone really hurt, or are they making a big fuss?
explain: Use the swap check. Put in the meaning «very hard to bear» and see if it fits. It fits «Maths homework is agony», so the speaker is exaggerating. «He was in an agony of terror» is not about pain in the body: the words «of terror» tell you he was terribly frightened.
fact: Agony means very great pain of the body, or very great worry or sadness of the mind.
fact: «In agony» means in great pain, and it is often used in stories and medicine.
fact: «Agony aunt» (UK) means someone who answers readers' problems in a magazine.
fact: «In an agony of terror» means terribly frightened.
fact: In everyday talk people exaggerate: «The waiting was agony!» means it was very hard to wait, not that anyone was hurt.
exercise: «She lay in agony after she twisted her ankle.» What does agony mean here? | A small, quiet cough | A kind of cake | *Very great pain of the body | Very happy and calm
exercise: Sam says, «Maths homework is agony!» What does Sam mean? | *Maths is very hard and annoying | Maths hurts his body | Maths homework has been cancelled | Maths is his favourite subject
exercise: «He was in an agony of terror.» Find and copy the word that tells you how he felt. | an | *terror | was | he
test: Which phrase means someone who answers readers' problems in a magazine? | in agony | the agony of waiting | *agony aunt | agony and terror
test: What does «the agony of waiting» most likely mean? | *Waiting that feels very hard to bear | A bad injury from a fall | A cold, wet day | A very fast train
exam: Agony is a noun, so which word is its plural form? | *agonies | agonia | agony's | agonys
